# data/guideline_text/ IS CONTENT-ADDRESSED. GIT MUST NOT REWRITE IT.
#
# Each .txt is a guideline document's own words, fetched once, and its .json
# sidecar records the sha256 of those exact bytes. `ingest_guideline_text_files.py`
# re-hashes the file and REFUSES on mismatch — that refusal is the guarantee
# that what the library quotes is what the publisher published.
#
# On 2026-09-08 all thirteen files failed that check on a Windows working tree.
# Nothing had been edited and `git status` was clean: git had materialised the
# LF bytes it stores as CRLF on checkout, so every file hashed differently from
# its sidecar while being byte-identical in the repository. The next run of the
# ingest would have refused the entire corpus, and the obvious "fix" — hashing
# with newlines normalised — would have quietly weakened the one check that
# makes the provenance chain worth having.
#
# -text disables all end-of-line conversion for these paths in both directions.
data/guideline_text/*.txt -text
data/guideline_text/*.json -text
